      "problem": "By expressing $\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ in the form $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{k}),$ determine the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3/4})\n$$\n\nHence $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$",
